 # Tile-based Rasterization with Radix Sort
 
-Also called: Tiled 2D Gaussian Splatting Rasterization with Radix Sort
+Also called: Tiled Rasterization with Global Radix Sort, Tile-based Gaussian Rasterization
 
 ## Overview
-Tile-based rasterization with radix sort is a GPU-parallel rendering method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that divides the image into tiles to manage memory and processing load, assigning Gaussians to tiles based on their projected axis-aligned bounding boxes [Parent]. It solves the performance bottleneck of per-pixel sorting by using a single global radix sort to achieve per-tile depth ordering, enabling real-time performance for millions of splats [S2, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that may perform redundant depth sorting or evaluate splats against irrelevant pixels, this method ensures that each tile is processed by one GPU thread block walking a single sorted list [S2, unofficial].
+Tile-based Rasterization with Radix Sort is a GPU-parallel rasterization method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that divides the image into tiles (typically $16 \times 16$ or $32 \times 32$ pixels) to manage memory and processing load, assigning Gaussians to tiles based on their projected 2D splats [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of per-pixel sorting by using a single global radix sort to bin splats by tile and sort them by depth simultaneously [S2, unofficial]. This sets it apart from non-tiled methods by enabling thread-block-level parallelism per tile and from sibling sorting methods by relying on a global sort key rather than per-ray or local approximations.
 
 ## Shared principles
-The method operates on a list of $N$ projected 2D Gaussian splats, each defined by center $(x_i, y_i)$, 2D covariance matrix $\Sigma'_i$, color $c_i$, opacity $\alpha_i$, and depth $Z_c$ [S2, unofficial]. The image is divided into a grid of tiles with dimensions $T_w \times T_h$ (typically $16 \times 16$ or $32 \times 32$ pixels) [S2, unofficial].
