 ### [Tile-Based Culling](2819-tile-based-culling.md)
-Tile-Based Culling is a method for computing a precise mapping between 3D Gaussians and image tiles to eliminate irrelevant tiles during rasterization. It solves the problem of overestimating the number of tiles a Gaussian intersects, which occurs when using standard axis-aligned bounding boxes (AABBs) that are overly conservative. Unlike the parent method, which assigns a Gaussian to every tile its projected splat touches (potentially including tiles with negligible opacity), Tile-Based Culling iteratively examines candidate tiles to verify actual overlap, thereby reducing the workload for the subsequent radix sort and rendering phases.
